            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** Around the attic hatch (can seal with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack")



** Around the door to the garage, which can be sealed with weatherstripping



** There is also some very minor leakage at the location where you removed your woodstove chimney



Please note that your home is very air tight already, and close to being under ventilated. Please see the section about ventilation in the additional comments of this report before addressing any of the air leakage locations. 



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $100 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.3 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.

</airSeal>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 85.2% efficient oil boiler. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE.



Savings: By replacing your current oil boiler with a 95% AFUE condensing propane boiler, you could potentially save $1,400 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 22.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). While updating your boiler, you should also install ECM circulation pumps and qualify for up to $175 in rebates per pump (up to 2 per fiscal year) from EEIP. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. In addition, LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Fail



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 10.6 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current boiler is direct vent and therefore not susceptible to back-drafting. 



During my assessment of your home, I saw smoke detectors but no carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed and operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

 

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - a range hood fan

        - two bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan that seals well, uses little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



As outlined in the Air Sealing section of this report, your house is already reasonably air tight. If your house was being built today, it would likely have a Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) installed. However, these can be harder to retrofit into older houses. You already have an outside fresh air supply pipe directly connected to the return air duct in your crawlspace. This fresh air duct has a manual damper that can be adjusted to regulate the amount of air coming into the house when your exhaust appliances are operating. I recommend that you manually adjust this damper to allow more fresh air into your home.



You will know if your house is too tight in the fall and winter if there is excessive condensation building up on the windows or if odours (cooking or other) linger for too long or if the house starts to smell a bit "stale". If so, operate your exhaust fans and consider opening the fresh air supply damper more. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife



The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $1.21 per liter 

Propane - $0.683 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



